    "judgment": {
      "status": "source_error",
      "reasoning": "All three final answers are mathematically correct and supported by their own captured solutions. For q-118755, the antiderivative is (1/2)ln(x²+4): the positive half-line integral diverges to +∞ and the negative half-line integral diverges to -∞. Thus I is false and II and III are true. Oddness gives a zero Cauchy principal value, but does not establish convergence of the improper integral. The source drops the factor 1/2 from the logarithmic limit in its final calculation for statement II; this does not change divergence. For q-51075, the antiderivative x³ shows both half-line integrals diverge to +∞, so II and III alone are true. Its third bullet incorrectly says 'Statement II is true' while discussing statement III; its concluding answer is correct. For q-118774, the antiderivative -e^{-x²} gives convergent half-line integrals -1 and 1, whose sum is 0. These authored errors affect the source explanations, not the final answer selections.",
