      "problem": "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nLet's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({a}^{3}-{0}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({x}^{3}{|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({0}^{3}-{a}^{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that ${a}^{3}\\to -\\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is 