    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-118774",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits",
      "problem": "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-{e}^{-{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}-(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}})) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
