      "math_academy_id": "q-6302",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region",
      "problem": "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}$ and the $x$-axis.",
      "worked_solution": "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={e}^{x}$,  then $\\text{d}u={e}^{x}\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{x}){|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{0}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡{e}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function.  Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{{e}^{-x}}{{e}^{-2x}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}}{{e}^{2x}\\cdot ({e}^{-2x}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
